  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\n- Chance for Isolated Thunderstorms This afternoon and evening\n\nLow stratocu continues to remain through the southern tier of\nMichigan with light southerly winds. Severe weather through \nIndiana continues to track to the south east with any outflow \nmoving northward running into a wall of stability. That stability \nwill stifle any convection this afternoon and evening through \nsouthern Michigan. The sole caveat will be convection from \nboundary interactions. Currently there is three different outflow \nboundaries moving in different directions that are set to \nintersect through southwest MI. There are some CAMS that suggest \nthat some convection could form. SFC based CAPE is around 1000 \nJ/KG along the I 94 corridor. However, there is upwards of 200 \nJ/KG of CIN. So while it is possible that convection could form \nthrough the south and will need to be watched, the stable air \ncould preclude storm development. \n\nThe better chance will be as a speed max moves from the east to \nsoutheast across central lower MI. Lapse rates in the tri city \narea are 6 to 7 C/km through the mid levels. Couple that with MU \nCAPE around 1400 J/KG and PWATS of 1.43 inches and you have a \ndecent environment for storm development. There is some cu \ndevelopment through central and norther lower. However, a large \ncirrus shield should limit daytime heating, though Max temps along\nthe US 10 corridor could get into the mid 80s. Latest CAMS \ncontinue to suggest the potential for isolated storms to be \npossible after 20Z. DCAPES are in the 800 to 900 J/KG range so \nDamaging winds are the main concern. Any shower and storm chances \nwill dwindle through the evening hours, \n\n- Foggy and chances for light rainfall through the south to end the work week\n\nAny rainfall and convection will add to the moisture throughout \nthe lower levels through southern Michigan. Calm winds, a stable \nair mass and moist lower levels is a great recipe for another \nround of fog through Thursday morning. Expect areas of fog to \ndevelop overnight. Fog could be denser than this morning's but \nwill hold off on any advisories for now. Another upper level \ntrough will drop down from the west tomorrow. The track of that \ntrough is trending towards this week's previous systems. They are \ntrending towards IN south into Ohio. The only precipitation could \nbe on the fringes along the I 94 corridor. Only light rain is \nexpected with a low chance for any thunder.\n\n- Precipitation Chances This Weekend\n\nThe next chance for widespread precipitation will be Saturday \nnight and Sunday as the pattern shifts and the zonal flow shifts\nnorthward bringing a mid level shortwave propagates over high\npressure building into the Great Plains. That short wave will \nbring decent surge of moisture Sunday. There is some question on \ntiming an position of the mid level trough. However there should \nbe good thickness advection with MUCAPE values in excess of 2000 \nJ/KG. So there is the persistent trend for the potential for \nstronger storms. Mid to long range models also continue to show a \n35 to 40 kt 850mb jet through Lower Michigan Sunday morning. This \nwill aid storm development Sunday.",
